name: repo-health-check
description: Use when the user asks to check if repos are clean, synced, green, or healthy - reads the precomputed health report at ~/.repo_health/latest.json (default scope ~/Code_2026) and offers to fix issues
---

# Repo Health Check

Triage and fix the repos flagged in `~/.repo_health/latest.json`. The deterministic scan (git status, ahead/behind, `gh run list`) runs on a launchd schedule (`com.v.repo-health` at 07:20 and 17:50, 10 min before gaa). **Do not re-run the scan** unless the report is missing, stale, or the user explicitly asks — read the JSON.

## When to Use

- User asks "check repos", "are repos clean", "all green?", "repo status"
- User wants to verify everything is committed, pushed, and synced
- After a batch of work across multiple repos

## Procedure

### 1. Read the report

```bash
cat ~/.repo_health/latest.json
```

Schema (per repo): `repo, path, branch, dirty, dirty_files, ahead, behind, has_upstream, ci, ci_detail, errors`. Top level has `scanned_at`, `root` (default `/Users/v/Code_2026`), `gh_available`, `repos[]`.

If the file is missing or `scanned_at` is older than ~12 h, ask the user before re-scanning. To re-scan:

```bash
python3 /Users/v/Code_2026/gaa/scripts/repo_health_scan.py        # full (with git fetch)
python3 /Users/v/Code_2026/gaa/scripts/repo_health_scan.py --no-fetch  # working-tree only, fast
```

### 2. Surface issues

A repo needs attention iff any of: `dirty == true`, `ahead > 0`, `behind > 0`, `ci in {failure, in_progress}`.

Print a concise table for the user covering only attention-needing repos. Skip the green ones. `ci == "none"` means no CI runs found for that branch — not a failure.

### 3. Fix issues (with judgement, not blindly)

| Condition | Default action | When to ask first |
|-----------|---------------|------------------|
| Dirty working tree | Stage, commit, push | Always — show the diff first; the user may be mid-experiment |
| Behind only | `git pull --ff-only` | If non-fast-forward, stop and ask |
| Ahead only | `git push` | If branch is unusual or unreviewed work, ask |
| Ahead + behind (diverged) | Stop. Ask. | Always — could need rebase or merge |
| CI failure | Report which workflow failed (`ci_detail` has the URL). Do NOT auto-fix. | Always |
| CI in_progress | Mention, don't block | Don't auto-act |

A single dirty repo with a clean diff and an obvious purpose can be committed and pushed in one go. Anything else — diverged branches, unexplained changes, CI failures — is exactly where the LLM step matters: think before acting.

### 4. Re-verify after fixing

Either re-run the scan (above), or just re-check the affected repos individually with `git status` / `git rev-list --left-right --count @{u}...HEAD`.

## Architecture

- **Scan (deterministic)** — `~/Code_2026/gaa/scripts/repo_health_scan.py`, scheduled via `~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.v.repo-health.plist` (07:20 + 17:50). Walks `~/Code_2026/*/.git`, writes `~/.repo_health/latest.json` atomically.
- **Brief (passive)** — `~/Code_2026/gaa/scripts/repo_health_brief.sh` is invoked by the SessionStart hook in `~/.claude/settings.json`; injects a one-line-per-problem-repo summary into context only when something is wrong, silent on green days.
- **Triage (this skill)** — reads the JSON, applies judgement on what to fix.

The scan is scheduled 10 minutes before gaa so gaa works on fresh repo state.

## Common Mistakes

- Re-running 26× `gh run list` live when the JSON already has `ci` per repo. Read the file.
- Auto-pushing a dirty repo without inspecting the diff. Always show the user what's being committed.
- Treating `ci == "in_progress"` as failure. It's just running.
- Treating `ci == "none"` as failure. It just means no CI runs were found for the branch (e.g. workflow not configured, or the branch hasn't been pushed).

